epartment: Interest on refunds of taxes\n- Other\n- District of Columbia\n\nI notice the 1955 row reveals some interesting details:\n- Military functions dominate at 35,532\n- Civil functions are significantly lower at 548\n- Health, Education, and Welfare shows 1,993\n- Treasury Department's interest on public debt is 6,370\n- Interest on tax refunds is comparatively small at 62\n\nThe spending patterns show Defense Department as the clear leader in expenditures.\n\nFocusing specifically on Defense Department spending, I can see two key components:\n- Military functions: 35,532\n- Civil functions: 548\n\nThis gives a total of 36,080, which represents the most substantial portion of governmental spending.\n\nI'm wrestling with interpreting the department's total spending. The columns suggest nuanced breakdown, leaving uncertainty whether to use the 35,532 or 36,080 figure.\n\nUltimately, 36,080 seems more precise - representing the complete Defense Department expenditure for 1955.\n","signature":""},{"type":"toolRequest","id":"call_2cc1493fa94a486b9e3c46d7","toolCall":{"status":"success","value":{"name":"shell","arguments":{"command":"cat /app/answer.txt"}}},"_meta":{"goose_extension":"developer"}}],"metadata":{"userVisible":true,"agentVisible":true}}}
